        "text": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p>Match the head and base to how you tie. The grid below is the stock we keep on the Ilwaco bench.<\/p><h3>Rotary vs fixed<\/h3><p>True rotary helps with palmering hackle, checking symmetry, and dubbing loops. A solid fixed head is enough if you mostly wrap in place and want fewer moving parts. Neither is &quot;better.&quot; It is how you tie.<\/p><h3>Jaws<\/h3><p>Midge jaws matter if you live on #18-#24. Streamer and salt hooks need a wider, stronger bite. If hooks spit out of the jaws, change jaws or vise before you change your whole pattern list.<\/p><h3>C-clamp vs pedestal<\/h3><p>C-clamp saves space and travels well if you have a desk edge. Pedestal stays put, moves around the bench, and gives you a place to park beads and hooks. Match the base to the room you tie in.<\/p><h3>Budget tiers<\/h3><p><strong>Starter:<\/strong> a vise that holds hooks securely and does not wobble. You can upgrade later. <strong>Mid:<\/strong> smoother rotary, better jaws, less fuss on long sessions. <strong>Premium:<\/strong> the vise you stop thinking about. Buy once if you already know you will keep tying.<\/p><h3>What we stock<\/h3><p>Look for Stonfo, Peak, Regal, Renzetti, and related bench brands in the grid below (availability moves). If you already know you want a Stonfo Elite, open that PDP directly. Scissors, bobbins, and the rest of the bench live under <a href=\"\/collections\/fly-tying-tools\">Fly Tying Tools<\/a>. Materials for the next bag sit under <a href=\"\/collections\/fly-tying-materials\">Fly Tying Materials<\/a>.<\/p>"
          }
        },
